the right  so this is the way you see  how the links are basically  they are created available to us  in a very simplified manner  okay and that's where  the hyperlinks are basically working  with us  welcome to this session on react.js  interview questions through this session  we will explore the most commonly posed  interview questions that you would face  if you walk up to a developer interview  on react.js let's start with going  through and exploring the general set of  react.js interview questions that would  be commonly asked in an interview the  first question would be what are the  features of react.js now right through  this session we would follow a common  methodology where we would explore the  question and then look at the best way  in which you could have answered that  question if posed to you during a  react.js interview so starting with the  first question what are the features of  react so react has many extensions for  complete application architecture  support jsx is used with react to  describe what the user interface should  typically look like react is broken into  small components so you look at the  complete application you want to build  break it down into individual components  and a component is typically a piece of  user interface like a function that  would return a stream of html characters  speedy performance is one of the core  features of react as only the updated  components need to be re-rendered on the  screen and not the entire page the  one-way data binding feature on the  other hand keeps everything modular and  fast the virtual dom is also a  lightweight representation of the real  dom which is again a feature which helps  speed up performance and avoids a full  page refresh and a full dom tree shake  which really speeds up performance so  what is jsx how do you answer this so  jsx is typically the extension used for  react and it's nothing but an extension  to the javascript programming language  because all that you can use inside your  browser for execution typically has to  be javascript because in html5 you  cannot have any third-party plugins  installed in the browser and that's the  reason the browser would only ship by  default with a javascript runtime engine  so by using jsx you can write html  structures in the same file that would  contain your javascript code as well  making the updates to the code very  simple so it's simple to update and make  modifications to your code because the  code and the data and the business logic  is all composed in a single file so  because the code and the business logic  is in a single file it makes uh updating  and incorporating change requests  to the code very simple and easy so it's  basically used with react to describe  what your user interface should look  like and it's what you typically would  term as the html template or the view  coming to the next question can jsx be  directly read by the web browser so web  browsers cannot read jsx directly now  why is that well the reason is simple  it's because they are only built to read  regular javascript objects and jsx is  not a regular javascript objects it's a  react component so for a web browser to  read a jsx file the file needs to be  transformed into a regular javascript  object and for this there is a  javascript api called people which is  internally used by react so what do you  understand by the virtual dom that we've  been speaking about in react so react  gives a very lightweight representation  of the real dom the actual dom structure  of the page so dom is document object  model it is an api created by w3c and it  creates an in-memory representation of  the html elements as an inverted tree  structure a balanced tree structure  which is the fastest search algorithm  when you want to search for html  elements and do a dynamic update on them  but again updating the basic dom tree  structure of the page causes a full page  refresh and if you only want to update a  specific component you wouldn't need the  entire page to be refreshed and that is  where react comes in it uses a virtual  dom which is a layer on top of the  actual dom which ensures that only a  certain part of the page is refreshed  and that speeds up performance so when  the state of an object changes virtual  dom changes only that object in the real  dom instead of updating all the objects  and this is what makes things really  fast so why use react instead of other  frameworks like angular first of all  it's very easy to create dynamic web  pages dhtml where you can update the  view or the template at runtime the  components are reusable so you reuse  rather than reinvent the performance is  great since you're working with the  virtual dom you don't need a full page  refresh the unidirectional data flow  makes data move one way there's no  two-way data binding by default and  that's the reason the page does not have  to do a lot of heavy lifting of  remembering whether the view is changing  whether the the base javascript entity  class is changing and that saves a lot  of uh processing time again to debug  react is much more simpler and that's  what makes it more popular in terms of  its simplicity and speed as compared to  other frameworks like angular what is  the difference between es5 and es6  standard so these are scripting  standards okay that are defined by the  european computer manufacturers  association or ecma and all of the  scripting languages are a subset of the  standard and are derived from these  standards to ensure that the code will  execute on all browsers and the code  that we are writing is standards based  so these are the few instances where es6  syntax has changed from es5 if you look  at the es5 first syntax at the top we're  creating a simple react class component  and the render function just returns a  stream of html content in ex6 there's a  new keyword that comes in which is  extends so you are extending from the  base uh react component and creating a  custom component and then returning a  stream of characters also the function  keyword no longer needs to be used so  the exports versus export also changes  so you use exports to export out class  from a module so that it can be accessed  from another module in es6 you simply  say export and the component require  versus import so you would require a  third party component or an api into  your javascript file to use it in es6  it's replaced with the import statement  very similar to the syntax that we use  in code java so how do you go and create  a react app first you install node.js as  a base platform which you can download  from the node.js website next any text  editor of your choice could be atom  visual studio code or sublime text third  you create a react apt using a command  prompt terminal depending on the os you  are using the command to give is create  hyphen react space my first app okay so  the command that you would give to  create a new react app is create hyphen  react hyphen app space and the name of  the application you would want to create  in this case it is my hyphen first  hyphen app and that could be a custom  name of your choice so what is an event  in react well on the web when you talk  about dynamic html it's all about  interacting with the user so the user  performs some interaction event with the  web page which could be a click of a  button a mouse move and these events in  action can be triggered by a user or any  system or could be pressing a key or a  mouse click etc and in response to that  event you would execute a block of code  which is called an event handler so here  we have class simple which is a custom  class that extends the base react  component the name of the function work  which triggers a simple alert message  box and a render function which returns  html when you click on the button we  would want to bind that to the event  handler this dot work and that's how you  connect a click event to an event  handler function what are synthetic  events in react well a synthetic event  is an object which acts as a cross  browser wrapper around the browser's  native event it combines the behavior of  different browsers native events into  one api which makes sure that the events  are consistent across different browsers  and this brings in the standards based  development to ensure in this digital  age the code that we are creating for  different browsers have consistency in  terms of its execution so here we see a  function action link we see a function  handle click which takes e as an event  parameter e dot prevent default ensures  that there is no error that the browser  is raising in case the event function  call is not directly compatible with  that specific browser so prevent default  is used to suppress any browser specific  errors or crashes and a simple  console.lock which will lock into the  developer tools window which says you  just clicked a link and then we've got  the return where we are returning the  view to or the html template where on  click we connect to this event handler  called handle click so handle click is  basically a wrapper and the entire  action link is a wrapper object which is  a synthetic event we don't directly go  and bind to the on click and then  associate a function with it we actually  associate a synthetic object with it  which binds the base default event that  could be specific to a browser so some  browsers would probably understand an on  click sun another browser would  understand a click and to ensure there  is consistency of syntax and from a  development perspective the code that we  write will run on all browsers we need  to take care of using the synthetic  events which isolate the developer out  from the complexity of writing code  specific to different browsers so you  just write generic code which will work  on different browsers so how do lists  work in react so the list display data  in an ordered format the traversal 